As discussed, please see the attached notes on quantum from Akin from February 2025. See in particular pages 16-18 of the “White Paper” and pages 23-25 of the “Overview” document (one appears to have been
 lifted from the other). 
&nbsp;
From a quick review, under regulation 27I(4), where the price is up to £5,000 there is a fixed refund, according to the seriousness of the prohibited practice: more than minor: 25%; significant: 50%; serious:
 75%; or very serious: 100%. Where the price was over £5,000, regulation 27I(6) applies if there is clear evidence that the contract price exceeded the “market price” when the contract was made, the discount is based on that difference, and applies to all past
 and future payments (regulation 27I(3)(b) and (6)-(7)). 
&nbsp;
It looks like our primary position is that regulation 27I(4) applies because there is no market for vehicles that contain PDDs (I assume we would argue that the fixed refunds should apply to the price paid,
 despite it being over £5k). However, it looks like AKKC’s view is that regulation 27I(6) is more likely to be applicable in this case - see footnote 39 of the White Paper which reads:

&nbsp;
Albeit not in the NOx Litigation, we note that A Kramer’s view in the Note dated 15 March 2017 was supporting the alternative, i.e. “Although I can see reasons for preferring
 regulation 27I(4)-(5), I think it fairly unlikely that in a case of this size, in which so much evidence will be led (by VW if no one else) as to the effect of the defeat device on performance and legality and how easily it can be fixed, that the court will
 end up with the primarily small claims damages provisions in regulation 27I(4)-(5) of banded discounts, rather than the difference in value measure of regulation 27I(6)-(7), on the basis that there is not ‘clear evidence’ of the market price. Regulation 27I(4)-(5)
 is likely to be treated as only really suitable for small claims or cases in which the market price was no lower than the actual price (e.g. pressure selling albeit at a fair price, lies about a collateral financing deal etc) and after a trial of this magnitude
 I think it unlikely that the court will conclude that there is no clear evidence and therefore it has to use the blunt instrument of Regulation 27I(4)-(5).”.

&nbsp;
I’m not sure if things have moved on since then as I’ve not really been involved. Copying Tom who was involved in quantum around that time (but equally has had very little involvement recently). I’ve not seen
 AKKC’s note dated 15 March 2017 – Tom is having a look but doesn’t think he has seen it either.

&nbsp;
Please do let me know if you want us to do any more digging.

&nbsp;
Thanks, 
&nbsp;
Ollie 
