See below for Clyde’s advice on the Bingham email. The attached email further considers @Neil Pigott’s queries sent earlier.&nbsp;In summary, Charlie confirms yet again that we do not need their consent, but the offer of a meeting to explain our position is clearly the most pragmatic way forward. &nbsp;Thank you R&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Raminder&nbsp;GrewalGlobal&nbsp;HR&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;718From: Urquhart, Charles &lt;charles.urquhart@clydeco.com&gt;Date: Monday, 6 October 2025 at 10:23To: Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Riceman, Sarah &lt;Sarah.Riceman@clydeco.com&gt;Subject: RE: LD Agency [CC-UNITEDKINGDOM.131211.10761935.FID8873487]CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Good morning Raminder,&nbsp;I have now had a chance to look at this. My thoughts are below:-&nbsp;General Advice&nbsp;My advice is as follows:-&nbsp;Clearly in a perfect world obtaining an employee’s consent/ buy in to any sort of change is always preferable – however in my view this is not necessary here and I entirely understand and appreciate why we would not want to have to do this in any event;The reason why consent is not necessary is:-Clause 4.2(c) of their contracts of employment requires each of them during their employment to “comply with all reasonable and lawful directions given to the Employee by the Company” – in my view working with Martyn Day on ongoing litigation which they have been working hand in glove with him on anyway for several years for a trial that starts next week is a reasonable and lawful direction;Clause 4.2(b) of their contracts of employment requires each of them during their employment to “diligently exercise such powers and perform such duties as may from time to time be assigned to the Employee by the Company;” – this is the same point.Although my view is that Clauses 4.2 (b) and (c) adequately cover the point, there is an additional helpful provision at Clause 4.1 of their contracts which states “The Employee shall serve the Company as a Senior Associate and will report to Legal Director or their nominee.” As such Martyn Day could be designated as the “nominee” for the purposes of their contracts.Please note that there are additional relevant clauses in their contracts which they should be complying with e.g. “to use their best endeavours to promote, protect, develop and extend the business of the Company.” Their actions are, in my view, in clear breach of this.Sarah Bingham’s reference to Clause 4.2(a) in her email is correct albeit she has spun the meaning for her own purposes – the business of the Company remains exactly as it was before and taking some direction from Martyn Day for the common good does not change that;The concept of working on a case with other individuals outside of the firm giving directions to them on a piece of litigation is hardly a novel concept – for example, presumably these individuals do work at the behest of external counsel? No consent is required for those arrangements.&nbsp;Pallas’s Questions&nbsp;Turning specifically to Pallas’s email of 8.39pm last evening and the relevant questions for us:-&nbsp;3. I agree entirely with Pallas’s paragraph 3. If the employees were being seconded they would need to give specific consent but this is not a secondment and falls within the confines of their contracts of employment as explained above;5.2. Given JLG and Gallagher’s behaviour to date, notwithstanding that Nick Johnson confirmed in writing to Alicia that he agreed to the case leads remaining employed by PG until the end of the trial, their ongoing behaviour suggests that they may still be manouevring to escape early. It is therefore essential that all communications with them continue the tone of Anna’s email to them of Saturday 4 October – i.e. respectful/ open to hearing their concerns (however ridiculous they may be) and avoiding any opportunity for them to argue that PG have somehow breached their contracts of employment. That is not to say that we cannot respectfully push back on their points. Pallas’s suggestion of a meeting is eminently sensible albeit that PG should bear in mind that the case leads may try and get a rise out of PG in that meeting on purpose to argue that there has been a break down in relations. On this point, given that the trial starts next week, is there any world now where the case leads could resign and hope to be working at JLG next week for the trial? Something to bear in mind. Something else to consider, if not already done, would be to offer each employee access to PG’s Employee Assistance Programme if it has one – this is because the employees say that they are all stressed – and would therefore demonstrate that you are acknowledging their “stress”.&nbsp;AA Agreement&nbsp;I understand that the word “solely” has been removed from the document. This is helpful in that it deals with one of their points. It also reduces the scale of the delegation of authority to LD which in turn reduces the scope for them to argue that there has been a breach of contract – albeit as explained above I don’t think there has been any such breach;Although I am loathe to suggest any more changes to the Agency agreement given that this needs to be approved by Leigh Day ASAP, in a perfect world a further enhancement could be to designate Martyn Day as the “nominee” in terms of the new reporting line of the Case Leads – so that the language matches up to their contracts of employment. This is not essential however for the purposes of this Agency agreement – it would be enough to tell the employees that Martyn is the person &nbsp;- or one of the people - they need to report to;Otherwise I have no more comments on the Agency agreement.&nbsp;I hope that this helps but if you or Pallas or anyone else have any queries please do not hesitate to ask.&nbsp;Best wishes&nbsp;Charlie&nbsp;PS Just seen your latest email come in. I will review that now&nbsp;Charles&nbsp;UrquhartPartner&nbsp;|&nbsp;Clyde&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Co&nbsp;LLPDirect&nbsp;Dial:&nbsp;+44&nbsp;20&nbsp;7876&nbsp;4215&nbsp;|&nbsp;Mobile:&nbsp;+44&nbsp;7810&nbsp;607&nbsp;355The St Botolph Building | 138 Houndsditch | London EC3A 7AR | UKMain +44 207 876 5000 | Fax +44 333 3000 232&nbsp;| www.clydeco.com​My pronouns are: he / him​&nbsp;Follow&nbsp;us&nbsp;on&nbsp;twitter&nbsp;@clydecoemp​​​​&nbsp;From: Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 06 October 2025 07:57To: Urquhart, Charles &lt;charles.urquhart@clydeco.com&gt;; Riceman, Sarah &lt;sarah.riceman@clydeco.com&gt;Subject: Fw: LD Agency&nbsp;Hi. &nbsp;Pls see the &nbsp;Pallas queries on the Employment related points within Bingham’s email. Thank you. R&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for iOS​​​​Raminder&nbsp;GrewalGlobal&nbsp;HR&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;718This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From:&nbsp;Matthew Getz &lt;Matthew.Getz@pallasllp.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Sunday, October 5, 2025 8:39 pmTo:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: LD Agency &nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Alicia, this is our recommendation beneath. In short:&nbsp;a.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; We can’t totally ignore the case leadsb.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tell LD (and counsel) the AA can’t be finalised before the PTRc.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Speak to LD after the PTR to make necessary changesd.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Then speak to case leads to tell them about changes, and for the rest they need to get with the picture&nbsp;The emails back and forth with this team is getting us nowhere. We think there needs to be a meeting with them, attended by Alicia and Anna. That obviously can’t be before the PTR, so would need to be on Tuesday at the earliest. But it might even have to be Wednesday. We would prepare you for this.Their overwhelming issue is the delegation of work by LD to individual solicitors at PG. This has always seemed problematic to us, but LD would not budge. We presumed that in practice, things would continue to work as they have done up to now, and Martyn Day seemed to confirm that – but the language remains different.To be clear, we are not conceding the principle that the case leads have the right to consent to this agreement – but they can’t just be ignored.We consider (but should get it cleared by Clydes) that there is nothing inherently wrong or out of the ordinary with an employer telling an employee to do, for a limited period and for specific work, what a responsible third party tells it to do – as long as the employer continues to oversee and supervise (including in the SRA sense) the employee. So if the employer considers the employee is nor being directed properly, it can tell the employee not longer to follow the directions of the third party.But that situation is not precisely what we have, because of the word “solely” in the Agency Agreement, where it relates to LD’s supervision of PG personnel doing the Common Work delegated to them. That makes it appear that only LD will know what the PG employees will do, so PG can no longer supervise them.So what is the way forward? We suggest the following:Tell LD (and counsel) now that we will not be able to agree the AA before the PTR, and that we will need to speak to them after the PTR. During that discussion, give them a summary of the case leads’ concerns, remind them that they want and need to work with the case leads, and the only way we can see this getting anywhere is if we drop the word “solely”. We must also tell them that (subject to Clydes agreeing this is acceptable) PG will let the employees know it is authorising them to do the work at LD’s direction on a limited basis (Common Work only), that PG will retain oversight and supervision, and if PG considers that any direction is improper (eg, a breach of regulations), it will have the right to rescind our instructions to the employees to follow LD’s directions. We see this as the only way forward. Since Natasha has already been in contact with Martyn, it may be that she needs to deliver this message.At the same time, have Clydes look at the case leads’ letter and the agency agreement (with and without the word “solely”) and get their view on the employment law aspects (and how far we can push things). Clydes should also confirm that they see no employment issues with the way we are proposing to resolve this (which should not be an issue). Raminder can draft an employment response, but it should not&nbsp;be sent until we have reviewed it and determined if it should be sent, consistent with our strategy.When you meet the case leads on Tuesday (?), therefore, you will hopefully be able to say PG has amended the agreement, it is clear PG remains their supervisors, and you can be super-robust about everything else.We would also suggest that after that meeting, you have a follow-up with them and Martyn Day, but we have to make sure Martyn is on side before suggesting that.As for the PTR, there is no&nbsp;way we can agree the agency agreement (and any side letter) before then, so we will need to tell the court we’re in the final stages of agreeing it.Ben Williams KC should be told that the agency agreement is being finalised –if necessary, he can say that is being done in consultation with the case leads. He can say that the case leads will be working their notice, which ends 15/19 Dec, but shouldn’t say anything more positive than that.ThanksMatt&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Matthew&nbsp;Getz​​​​PartnerT: +44&nbsp;20&nbsp;4574&nbsp;1006|M: +44&nbsp;79&nbsp;8538&nbsp;1095E:&nbsp; matthew.getz@pallasllp.com110&nbsp;Bishopsgate,&nbsp;London,&nbsp;EC2N&nbsp;4AYpallasllp.com&nbsp;|&nbsp;LinkedInFrom:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;05 October 2025 19:13To:&nbsp;Matthew Getz &lt;Matthew.Getz@pallasllp.com&gt;; Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: LD Agency&nbsp;Hi Matt,&nbsp;@Raminder Grewal&nbsp;has been provided with a copy of the email from Bingham. I don’t think there are any employment issues she’s raised that concern us but she can correct me if I’m wrong.&nbsp;Anna is offline at the moment and I don’t want to bother her but in your consideration can you also please advise as to whether we need to let Ben Williams know that the case leads continue to be upset and he shouldn’t make any positive statements about them tomorrow morning?&nbsp;Many thanks&nbsp;A&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for iOS​​​​Alicia&nbsp;AliniaChief&nbsp;Executive&nbsp;Officer+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;629This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic "}

