 Alessia de Quincey &lt;alessia.dequincey@pallasllp.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matthew Getz &lt;Matthew.Getz@pallasllp.com&gt;; Dougie Inglis &lt;dInglis@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Holme, Chris &lt;Chris.Holme@clydeco.com&gt;; Riceman, Sarah &lt;Sarah.Riceman@clydeco.com&gt;; Fergal Cathie &lt;Fergal.Cathie@clydeco.com&gt;; Lawrence, Jonathan &lt;Jonathan.Lawrence@clydeco.com&gt;Subject: Revised table and draft settlement agreement [CC-UNITEDKINGDOM.131211.10761935.FID8873487]&nbsp;Dear all,&nbsp;Please see below an updated table.&nbsp;Please also find attached a revised draft of the settlement agreement with suggested changes tracked. I would be grateful if you could review this tomorrow morning and feed back any comments/ amendments/ answers to points in the margins or in yellow highlight ahead of our call so we can minimise points to change tomorrow afternoon if we get as far as marking this up for review by Jones Chase.&nbsp;&nbsp;IssueOur current positionAlternative options 1.Termination Date[Friday 26 September or 3 October 2025][instructions]2.Salary and benefitsYour client would be paid his salary and benefits up and until the Termination Date whereupon they would cease;N/A3.Holiday payYour client would receive payment in respect of any accrued but untaken holiday days;N/A4.Notice periodOur client is prepared to release your client from his 6 month notice period on the proviso that your client waives any entitlement to any further salary, benefits, bonus or incentive from Pogust Goodhead;N/A5.Non-competitionOur client is prepared to release your client from the non-competition restrictive covenants at Clauses 22.2(a) 22.2(b) and the first sentence of Clause 22.2(e) of his service agreement dated 1 November 2023 (the Service Agreement) only in respect of the named law firm that he intends to move to;N/A6.Non solicitation of employeesYour client would be expected to reconfirm his commitment to comply in full with the non-solicitation of employees clause at Clause 22.2(c) (corrected cross reference from our original letter) of his Service Agreement save that the obligation would be extended to cover both Key Employees (as defined) and all other fee earners (qualified solicitors, barristers and paralegals) at Pogust Goodhead;&nbsp;Update: We did discuss a list of Key Employees/employees PG would want covered. Although this is a good idea – it will be easier to agree a blanket ban otherwise there could be haggling over particular employees/ levels of employee particularly in the time available They might push back on the other fee earners saying that is not in his contract. “Key Employee” only includes those with supervisory or strategic responsibility for Group Litigation only.Alternatively they might say that they will accept the additional restriction for consideration. They could also seek to accept the wider covenant for a reduced period.7.Non dealing with clientsYour client would be expected to reconfirm his commitment to comply in full with the non-dealing with clients’ clause at Clause 22.4 of his Service Agreement, and the definition of Key Clients shall be amended to make clear that this comprises Pogust Goodhead’s 750,000 NOx emission clients;&nbsp;Update: to include without limitation – each PG manufacturer claimant committee. The definition of Key Client excludes clients with whom he dealt on a minimal basis – which presumably could be all of the Nox emission clients. It is possible that they would be caught by the limb re Confidential Information but ideally we would extend this as requested Alternatively they might say that they will accept the additional restriction for consideration. They could also seek to accept the wider covenant for a reduced period.8.Non solicitation of clients and client committeeYour client would be expected to agree to a provision regarding the non-solicitation of clients’ for 12 months post theTermination Date, which will cover both the direct solicitation of Pogust Goodhead’s750,000 NOx emission clients but also indirect solicitation of those clients via the claimant client committee for the NOx emission litigation;&nbsp;Update: to include without limitation – each PG manufacturer claimant committee.There is no non solicitation of clients clause in his contract of employmentThey may argue that if he agrees to the non dealing clause why do we also need a non solicitation clause – the answer is because he could solicit the clients via the client committee and then others could deal with themThey might say that they will only accept the additional restriction for consideration.They could also seek to accept the wider covenant for a reduced period.9.New employer covenantsOur client would expect your client’s new employer to also enter into 12 month non solicitation and non dealing restrictive covenants from the Termination Date that it will not:-solicit or deal with Pogust Goodhead’s NOx emission clients subject to the standard de-duplication processes that have already been agreed in respect of clients generally.solicit Pogust Goodhead’s NOx emission clients via each PG manufacturer claimant committeeneither employ nor make any offer of employment to any fee earners (qualified solicitors, barristers and paralegals) at Pogust Goodhead; andnot attempt to join any Steering Committee or become a Lead Solicitor in the NOx litigation for as long as your client is employed by the new employer.&nbsp;&nbsp;Given that we don’t trust Gallagher we need his new employer to give us some comfort10.ConfidentialityYour client would be expected to agree to comprehensive confidentiality provisions save that it would be accepted that your client could work as part of the broader claimant cohort as part of the ongoing NOx emissions matter;&nbsp;Update: The carve out is to be linked to the CRO. It is agreed that information or documents that are subject to a Confidentiality Ring Order (CRO) in any of the litigation in which the New Employer is a representative, and to which at least one employee of the New Employer is already working, will not be captured by the confidentiality obligation. I have added a clause into the attached settlement agreement but we may need some further detail on how this would work in practice11.Mutual confidentiality and non-derogatory provisionsThe parties would each enter into mutual confidentiality obligations governing the background to your client’sresignation and the terms of this agreement together with mutual non derogatory obligations covering your client, Pogust Goodhead and any group company thereof and Pogust Goodhead’s directors and employees and those of any group company;&nbsp;Update: Non-derogatory obligations with Gallagher only. N/A12.Waiver of claimsYour client would be expected to provide a full waiver of any and all claims against Pogust Goodhead and its directors and employees (to include, withoutlimitation, employment, defamation and personal injury claims). Our client cannot agree to a full waiver of claims against your client and his future employer in circumstanceswhere (i) it does not even know the identity of the new employer; and (ii) it does not have full sight of your client’s nor any third party’s actions and breach of obligations relating tothis matter. It is prepared however to agree not to issue an injunction application againstyour client to hold him to his garden leave and post termination restrictive covenants;&nbsp;Update: Starting point is to receive a waiver of claims from Gallagher only. Second position is for PG to also waive any known claims. No waiver of claims against new Employer.They will almost certainly demand a full waiver against Gallagher/ the new employerIt is difficult to grant this without knowing what he has doneThere could be a significant damages claim against Gallagher/ NLJHowever- what is the price of peace? If he agrees to not attack PG on Nox litigation/ not take any more staff for 12 months is that sufficient price to pay for what he has done?13.Compensation paymentOur client has not acted in repudiatory breach of your client’s Service Agreement therefore no compensation is payable whatsoever;&nbsp;Update: If he agreed to all the restrictions etc – we would consider a small payment. For context: initial WP letter requested 12 months pay + £250k. Later offer revised to £120kThis might depend on their other asks and whether we need to pay something to extend his covenants14.Data Subject Access RequestYour client would be expected to withdraw his data subject access requested dated 10 September 2025 and undertake not to reissue another one; andN/A15.Legal FeesOur client is not aware of the quantum of your client’s legal fees. Our client may be prepared to consider making a contribution towards these fees if a deal can be reached along the lines set out in this letter and if you provide us with the quantum of such fees from 3 September 2025 onwards.&nbsp;This might depend on their other asks and whether we need to pay something to extend his covenants&nbsp;&nbsp;The additional points that they raised in their letter of 24 September 2025&nbsp;16.Cross referenceThis should be to Clause 22.2(c)We can agree this17.Client committeeIs there a client committee for the Nox emissions litigation – if so how is it best defined?Grateful for instructions18.De-duplication If the new employer already has claims in one or more of the 13 NOx group actions, it will be party to a de-duplication agreement with PG and the other claimant firms pursuant to which it may approach duplicate NOx claimants directly about their preference over which firm ultimately represents them. Please explain how your client intends to make this proposal compatible with those de-duplication agreements&nbsp;Update: New Employer covenants will be subject to the standard de-duplication processes that have already been agreed in respect of clients.I believe this process has been agreed whereby the firm that wrote to each individual first get to write to them to check whether that individual wishes to proceed with that firm or another &nbsp;– so presumably we can add in a carve out to reflect this wording in the agreement19.Multiple actionsA number of claimants have followed a practice where they have different NOx claims signed up with multiple claimant firms; for example the same claimant has one Mercedes claim with PG and another Mercedes claim with Leigh Day. Please confirm how your client would make its proposal compatible with that situation&nbsp;Update: Where a client has multiple claims and only one of those claims is with the Company, the New Employer covenants will not apply in respect of the New Employer soliciting or dealing with those clients in respect of claims that are not already with the Company.&nbsp; Grateful for instructions20.Confidential InformationThey have asked whether this needs to be more stringent than set out in contract of employmentProbably not - subject to discussion&nbsp;Finally, I decided against circulating a draft agreement ahead of the meeting as I don’t want them to then avoid the meeting by saying that they will simply review the agreement.&nbsp;Best wishes,&nbsp;Charlie&nbsp;Charles&nbsp;UrquhartPartner&nbsp;|&nbsp;Clyde&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Co&nbsp;LLPDirect&nbsp;Dial:&nbsp;+44&nbsp;20&nbsp;7876&nbsp;4215&nbsp;|&nbsp;Mobile:&nbsp;+44&nbsp;7810&nbsp;607&nbsp;355The St Botolph Building | 138 Houndsditch | London EC3A 7AR | UKMain +44 207 876 5000 | Fax +44 333 3000 232&nbsp;| www.clydeco.com​My pronouns are: he / him​&nbsp;Follow&nbsp;us&nbsp;on&nbsp;twitter&nbsp;@clydecoemp​​​​&nbsp;_____________________________________________________ If our account details change, we will notify these to you by letter, telephone or face-to-face and never by email. Clyde &amp; Co’s privacy notice can be found here Privacy notice: Clyde &amp; Co (clydeco.com) Please could we request that all correspondence with us is sent electronically where possible. For matters subject to the jurisdiction of England &amp; Wales and where you have agreed with the lawyer with the conduct of the case that email service is appropriate, please use this address service@clydeco.com for service, subject to the restrictions set out here . Please copy your email to the lawyer with conduct of the case and include our Clyde &amp; Co reference. This email is sent for and on behalf of Clyde &amp; Co LLP, a limited liability partnership registered in England and Wales under number OC326539 and with its registered office at The St Botolph Building, 138 Houndsditch, London, EC3A 7AR, United Kingdom (Tel: +44 20 7876 5000. Fax: +44 20 7876 5111). Clyde &amp; Co LLP is authorised and regulated by the Solicitors Regulati"}

