any numbers 10201109, 10925342 and 14305681 respectively) and is a law firm authorised and regulated by the Solicitors Regulatory Authority (SRA) under firm number 647886. &nbsp;“Heminsley Law” is a trading name/style of Heminsley. It should be noted that while the owners/directors of the companies in the partnership are not, technically, themselves partners, Heminsley refers to them as ‘non-member partners’ or as simply ‘partners’.&nbsp; A list of these directors/non-member partners is available for inspection at our registered office, which is at Enterprise House, 656 Chester Road, Birmingham B23 5TE.&nbsp; VAT registration number: 293 7561 64. &nbsp;&nbsp;From: Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 10 December 2025 15:16To: Jemma O'Reilly &lt;Jemma.Oreilly@heminsleylaw.com&gt;Subject: Legally Privileged &amp; Confidential&nbsp;Hi Jemma,&nbsp;Thanks for reviewing the other email for me earlier today – much appreciated. Please see the email chain below. Are you able to review the draft reply below too please?&nbsp;Hi Michael,&nbsp;Thank you for your emails and for outlining your concerns regarding Occupational Sick Pay (OSP). I can confirm the firm’s position regarding Occupational Sick Pay (OSP) as follows:&nbsp;Your contract makes it clear that any entitlement to pay during absence is subject to the firm’s sickness policy. That policy is not contractual, and we are not obliged to individually notify employees of its content. Employees are expected to review policies, which are made available from time to time on HiBob. The applicable policy, in place prior to your resignation and your current period of sick leave, governs any entitlement to OSP and SSP.The sickness policy uploaded on 17 November 2025 at 09:59:39 was the first formal iteration of a written sickness policy. No previous written sickness policy was in place.Condition 7(5) relating to OSP was included in this first iteration. The policy was published to clarify eligibility for occupational sick pay going forward.The firm confirms that no employee currently working their notice is in receipt of OSP under the terms of the policy.&nbsp;In line with this, the firm maintains that you are not eligible for OSP during your notice period and any entitlement is limited to Statutory Sick Pay (SSP).&nbsp;Kind regards,Natasha &nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Natasha&nbsp;Geraldo&nbsp;BairdHead&nbsp;of&nbsp;HR+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;643This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From: Michael Burke &lt;burke7y@hotmail.com&gt; Sent: 10 December 2025 15:03To: Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Re: M Burke&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Hi Natasha,&nbsp;I am sorry to chase again, but time is fast running out if I am to return to work, against medical advice, in order to receive full pay. The lack of response is potentially prejudicing my ability to mitigate my loss.&nbsp;&nbsp;If you can also please clarify the company's position on my unilateral contract change that would be helpful.&nbsp;Similarly, can you confirm the position re the courier who is due to arrive on Friday?&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for AndroidFrom: Michael Burke &lt;burke7y@hotmail.com&gt;Sent: Tuesday, December 9, 2025 5:43:13 PMTo: Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Re: M Burke &nbsp;Hi Natasha,&nbsp;Could you come back to me on the below at your earliest opportunity or let me know when you will be able to respond?&nbsp;&nbsp;As advised before, this is quite pressing.&nbsp;Given that we are now on day 7 of my absence (meaning the firm's position as I understand it is that I am only entitled to SSP for those 7 days as opposed to OSP), if the firm maintain that position then I will be forced to consider what options are open to me to dispute those 7 days, and any further that I assert should be paid at OSP and not SSP.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for AndroidFrom: Michael Burke &lt;burke7y@hotmail.com&gt;Sent: Monday, December 8, 2025 7:13:08 PMTo: Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Re: M Burke &nbsp;Hi Natasha,&nbsp;Thank you for this and for that information.&nbsp;Bearing all of this in mind i.e. that the policy has been changed without notification, that the policy constitutes (as you have termed it) part of my contractual framework, and that the policy makes a material change to my contractual position, is it still the firm's position that I am not eligible for OSP?&nbsp;&nbsp;I note that the firm, as is any employer, is obliged to notify their employees of any change in their contractual position.&nbsp;Kind regards,Michael&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for AndroidFrom